A pause must stop decoding video (review H2, and #404's V1 note on the NAS's CPU). The
animation chain switches clips by loading the next one into the hidden video and, once it
has decoded its first frame, bringing it to the front and playing it. A switch that began
just before the page paused the card, and finished loading during the pause, was still
played: a looping clip (a stage with one animation, or the sleep loop) then decoded for the
whole pause, under the local console's screen saver too. The chain now reads the page's
pause: such a clip goes to the front without playing, and the end of the pause plays it, as
it plays any frame the freeze held. A command it carried is reported cancelled with dsh-pet's
reason `frozen`, as a freeze reports the one playing. A lock alone changes nothing here:
dsh-pet lets explicit commands play under a lock.

